  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "A rectangle with a diagonal of length \\(x\\) is twice as long as it is wide. What is the area of the rectangle?",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(x^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{2}x^2\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2005-p4.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let the width be \\(w,\\) so the length is \\(2w.\\) Then \\(x^2 = w^2 + (2w)^2 = 5w^2,\\) giving \\(w^2 = \\dfrac{x^2}{5}.\\) The area is \\(w \\cdot 2w = 2w^2 = \\dfrac{2}{5}x^2.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Let the width be \\(w;\\) then the length is \\(2w\\)",
        "By the Pythagorean theorem \\(x^2 = w^2 + (2w)^2;\\) solve for \\(w^2\\) and then find the area \\(2w^2\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
